{
  "title": "What's this?",
  "title_target": "מַה זֶּה?",
  "goal": "Point at and name things around you, recognize the gender of nouns, and attach the article הַ־.",
  "bekal": [
    "ask what something you don't know is called",
    "tell masculine nouns from feminine ones",
    "tell \"a book\" from \"the book\""
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "שִׂיחָה — בַּחֲנוּת סְפָרִים",
      "intro": "Budi and Noa are in the campus bookshop. Budi points at things one by one.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "נֹעָה, מַה זֶּה?",
          "baca": "no'ah, mah zeh?",
          "id": "Noa, what's this?",
          "literal": "noa, what this?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "נֹעָה:",
          "text": "זֶה מִלּוֹן. מִלּוֹן עִבְרִי־אַנְגְּלִי.",
          "baca": "zeh milon. milon ivri-angli.",
          "id": "It's a dictionary. A Hebrew-English dictionary.",
          "literal": "this dictionary. dictionary hebrew-english."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "וְזֹאת? זֹאת מַחְבֶּרֶת?",
          "baca": "vezot? zot makhberet?",
          "id": "And this one? Is this a notebook?",
          "literal": "and-this (f)? this (f) notebook?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "נֹעָה:",
          "text": "כֵּן. מַחְבֶּרֶת גְּדוֹלָה.",
          "baca": "ken. makhberet gdolah.",
          "id": "Yes. A big notebook.",
          "literal": "yes. notebook big (f)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "לָמָּה גְּדוֹלָה וְלֹא גָּדוֹל?",
          "baca": "lamah gdolah velo gadol?",
          "id": "Why gdolah and not gadol?",
          "literal": "why big (f) and-not big (m)?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "נֹעָה:",
          "text": "כִּי מַחְבֶּרֶת הִיא מִלָּה נְקֵבָה.",
          "baca": "ki makhberet hi milah nekevah.",
          "id": "Because makhberet is a feminine word.",
          "literal": "because notebook she word feminine."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "אָה. וְאֵיךְ אֲנִי יוֹדֵעַ?",
          "baca": "ah. ve'eikh ani yode'a?",
          "id": "Oh. And how do I know?",
          "literal": "ah. and-how I know?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "נֹעָה:",
          "text": "בְּדֶרֶךְ כְּלָל ־ָה אוֹ ־ֶת בַּסּוֹף — נְקֵבָה.",
          "baca": "bederekh klal ah o et basof — nekevah.",
          "id": "Usually if it ends in -ah or -et, it's feminine.",
          "literal": "in-way general ah or et in-the-end — feminine."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "וְהַסֵּפֶר הַזֶּה? כַּמָּה הוּא עוֹלֶה?",
          "baca": "vehasefer hazeh? kamah hu oleh?",
          "id": "And this book? How much is it?",
          "literal": "and-the-book the-this? how-much it goes-up?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "נֹעָה:",
          "text": "רֶגַע... שִׁשִּׁים שֶׁקֶל.",
          "baca": "rega... shishim shekel.",
          "id": "Just a moment... sixty shekels.",
          "literal": "moment... sixty shekel."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "בְּסֵדֶר. אֲנִי לוֹקֵחַ אֶת הַסֵּפֶר.",
          "baca": "beseder. ani loke'akh et hasefer.",
          "id": "OK. I'll take the book.",
          "literal": "good. I take (obj) the-book."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "נֹעָה:",
          "text": "וְהַמִּלּוֹן? הוּא בֶּאֱמֶת טוֹב.",
          "baca": "vehamilon? hu be'emet tov.",
          "id": "And the dictionary? It's really good.",
          "literal": "and-the-dictionary? it really good."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**זֶה** and **זֹאת** both mean \"this\", but you choose by **the gender of the thing** you're pointing at. זֶה for masculine things, זֹאת for feminine ones. If you don't know what the thing is yet — like Budi in line 1 — use זֶה; that's the default in the question מַה זֶּה?",
        "⚠️ **Every noun has a gender, with no exceptions and no reason.** סֵפֶר (book) is masculine, מַחְבֶּרֶת (notebook) is feminine. There's no logic to work out; there are just two clues from the word's shape (see the next box) and habit. **Learn every noun TOGETHER with its gender from day one** — fixing this habit a year later costs far more.",
        "**הַ־ = \"the\".** סֵפֶר = a book (any book); הַסֵּפֶר = the book. Like English \"the\", it goes in front — but it's glued on, and Hebrew has **no** word for \"a\".",
        "⚠️ **\"This book\" needs הַ־ TWICE.** הַסֵּפֶר הַזֶּה — literally \"the-book the-this\". Forgetting the second הַ־ is the most common A1 mistake. Compare: זֶה סֵפֶר (this is a book) ≠ הַסֵּפֶר הַזֶּה (this book).",
        "**כַּמָּה זֶה עוֹלֶה?** (*kamah zeh oleh?*) — \"how much is it?\", literally \"how much does this go up?\". The verb עוֹלֶה really does mean \"go up\" and also \"cost\". Learn the sentence as a chunk; unit 11 takes it apart.",
        "**רֶגַע** (*rega*) literally means \"a moment\", used exactly like \"just a sec\". The sweeter version: רֶגַע אֶחָד (*rega ekhad*, one second).",
        "**כִּי** (*ki*) = because. Notice that the answer is a full sentence: כִּי מַחְבֶּרֶת הִיא מִלָּה נְקֵבָה. The הִיא in the middle isn't \"she\" but a link — Hebrew uses a pronoun in place of \"is\" when both sides are nouns. You'll see it again in unit 6.",
        "**אֶת הַסֵּפֶר** — this is the אֶת from unit 2. Because the book is **definite** (it has הַ־), the object must be preceded by אֶת. If it's indefinite, אֶת disappears: אֲנִי לוֹקֵחַ סֵפֶר."
      ],
